(Start only after I write "Section 3 approved. Start SECTION 4, Step 1 only.")

ROLE
You are a senior full-stack engineer specialized in authentication and security.

IMPORTANT FACT (do not get this wrong)
"Continue with Google" (OAuth) does NOT send a verification code by email. Google verifies identity on its own sign-in page (password, 2-step verification). A code sent to an email is a SEPARATE mechanism (email OTP). That is why this section builds BOTH methods, and they must not be confused or merged into one fake flow.

GOAL
Add REAL authentication with two methods:
A) "Continue with Google" (real Google OAuth, no fake or simulated login)
B) "Sign in with email code": the user enters an email, a 6-digit verification code is actually sent to that inbox, and the user enters it to log in

RULES (active during Section 4)
- Do not change the learning logic, quiz content, or scoring. This section adds a login page and an auth layer, which is allowed. The login UI must follow the design system created in Section 2 (its tokens, fonts, icons, components).
- No secrets (client secrets, API keys, service account keys) in frontend code or in the repository. Use environment variables and give me a .env.example.
- Do not rewrite the app. Add the auth layer and connect it to the existing user/progress data.
- Stop at every [STOP].

FIRST: INSPECT MY STACK
Check whether the app has a backend. If it is frontend-only, recommend the simplest secure option (for example Firebase Authentication, which supports Google sign-in and email link/code flows) and explain why. Wait for my choice. [STOP]

METHOD A: GOOGLE SIGN-IN
- Use Google Identity Services or Firebase Auth with the Google provider.
- Verify the Google ID token on the SERVER (or via the Firebase SDK). Never trust a token decoded only on the client.
- Read email, name, and profile picture, and require email_verified = true.
- Create the user on first login, and reuse the account on later logins (match by Google user ID/sub, not by email alone).

METHOD B: EMAIL VERIFICATION CODE
- The user enters an email. Validate the format, then send a real email through a transactional email provider (Resend, SendGrid, Postmark, or Firebase). Tell me which provider you picked and why.
- Code: 6 digits, cryptographically random, valid for 10 minutes, single use.
- Store only a HASH of the code on the server, never the plain code.
- Security: rate limit sends (e.g., max 3 per email per 15 minutes and per IP), limit verification attempts (max 5, then invalidate the code), use constant-time comparison, and keep error messages generic so they don't reveal whether an account exists.
- Resend button with a 60-second cooldown timer, and a clear "check your spam folder" hint.
- The email template follows the adventure theme (use the palette from the Master Brief), with the code large and readable and a plain-text fallback included.
- If both methods use the same email, link them to ONE account.

SESSIONS AND ACCOUNT
- Secure sessions (httpOnly, secure, sameSite cookies, or Firebase session handling). Logout works everywhere, and protected routes redirect to login.
- If the app currently stores progress locally or as a guest, migrate that progress to the account on first login (ask the user to confirm with a friendly prompt).
- Show the user's name and avatar in the profile area.

LOGIN UI STATES
Loading, error (wrong or expired code, network error, popup blocked), success, and resend. Everything accessible (labels, focus order, keyboard, OTP input with autofill support via autocomplete="one-time-code" and paste support).

